#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fpms-npu-tune - NPU core policy and thermal reality, reported not assumed.

Usage:
    fpms-npu-tune                       human-readable report (CHANGES NOTHING)
    fpms-npu-tune --json                machine-readable, same data
    fpms-npu-tune --watch               monitor loop; logs ONLY on state change
    fpms-npu-tune --verify              print the exact commands that confirm
                                        every path this tool guesses at
    fpms-npu-tune --print-env           the config.env line this policy implies

    fpms-npu-tune --apply --core-mask   resolve the mask, write /run/fpms/npu.env
    fpms-npu-tune --apply --governor performance    pin NPU DVFS (costs power)
    fpms-npu-tune --apply --restore-governor        undo the above

Exit: 0 ok, 1 something is degraded or unknown, 2 a fault worth acting on.
Never nonzero in --watch: it is a monitor, not a gate.


WHY THIS EXISTS
===============
Two separate defects, both silent, both costing detection.

1. TWO THIRDS OF THE NPU IS IDLE.
   The RK3588S has three NPU cores, 6 TOPS total. `init_runtime()` called with
   no `core_mask` runs on ONE core. Nothing in the FPMS codebase has ever set a
   mask, so every inference this rover has ever run used a third of the
   hardware -- and nothing anywhere said so.

   Worse, the obvious-looking fix is wrong. `RKNN_NPU_CORE_AUTO` reads like
   "use everything"; in Rockchip's C API it means "automatically pick an IDLE
   core" -- singular. For a single-model workload that is still one core.
   Setting FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=auto believing it means "all" leaves you exactly
   where you started, with a config line that looks like the question was
   settled. This tool refuses to let that read as success.
   VERIFY ON HARDWARE:
     python3 -c "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite as R; print(R.NPU_CORE_AUTO, R.NPU_CORE_0, R.NPU_CORE_0_1_2)"

   And the value is a NAME, not a number. fpms-npud (agent 2) accepts exactly
   "0" | "0_1" | "0_1_2" | "auto" and refuses everything else, so
   FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=7 -- the natural spelling if you are thinking in bit
   masks, which is how the kernel and this tool think -- stops the daemon from
   starting. This tool speaks the daemon's vocabulary and flags any setting
   that would be refused, before the daemon has to.

2. RK3588 THROTTLES HARD AND SILENTLY.
   A first-inference benchmark number is meaningless on this SoC. Frequency
   drops, latency doubles, nothing logs. `bench_npu.py` measures 30 frames
   from cold and reports a per-frame breakdown that is true for exactly those
   30 frames. This tool exists so the thermal state behind any such number is
   visible at the time it was taken.


THE CORE-MASK DECISION, AND WHY IT GOES THE WAY IT DOES
=======================================================
There is a real trade-off and it is NOT "more cores is faster":

  - Mask per model, one core each (0x1 / 0x2 / 0x4): three models run
    genuinely concurrently. This is a THROUGHPUT win. It requires >=2
    inference requests in flight to win anything at all.
  - One model across all three (0x7): the graph is split across cores, which
    lowers SINGLE-INFERENCE LATENCY. Speedup is sublinear -- weights are
    duplicated per core and all three cores share one DDR bus -- and for a
    small model the split overhead can exceed the gain.

FPMS runs ONE model. `FPMS_CAMERA_FPS=6` with `FPMS_INFER_EVERY=2` is roughly
3 inferences per second: a 333 ms budget per inference, arriving one at a
time. There is no concurrency for throughput mode to exploit. Cores 1 and 2
would sit idle in mask-per-model, which is the defect we started with.

So the only lever this workload can pull is latency, and latency here is not
vanity: it is the AGE of a detection. At the shipped cruise speed of 0.18 m/s
the rover travels 18 mm per 100 ms of inference latency before a fire box has
a position attached to it. Lower latency also buys headroom against
FPMS_NPU_MAX_LATENCY_MS as the SoC heats up and clocks down -- which is the
window in which this rover stops detecting without announcing it.

=> POLICY: with FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK unset, this resolves to ALL THREE CORES --
   token `0_1_2` -- for the shipped one-model workload. That is also
   fpms-npud's own built-in default, so the two agree by construction; this
   tool's job is to say WHY, and to catch a config.env override that would
   quietly undo it.

   MEASURE ME, and this is a real gate, not a formality: yolo26n is a nano
   model. If agent 7's bench shows 0_1_2 latency is WORSE than 0 -- entirely
   possible when per-core split overhead dominates a small graph -- then set
   FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=0 explicitly in config.env and record the two measured
   numbers next to it. The policy is "spread one model for latency"; 0_1_2 is
   the value that policy currently implies, not an article of faith.
     bench:  /opt/fpms-os/npu/bench/fpms_npu_bench.py   (agent 7)

   The power argument does NOT overturn this. Three cores for a third of the
   time is roughly the same energy as one core for the whole time; what costs
   battery is sustained 100% duty, and at 3 inferences/second the NPU is idle
   the large majority of every second. Compare that with the governor decision
   below, where the power cost IS the deciding factor.


THE GOVERNOR DECISION GOES THE OTHER WAY
========================================
This tool can pin a `performance` DVFS governor. It does not do so by default
and the unit does not do so at boot, deliberately.

A performance pin holds the NPU at maximum frequency CONTINUOUSLY, including
the ~90% of every second this workload leaves it idle. What it buys is the
DVFS ramp on the leading edge of an inference -- microseconds to a few
milliseconds -- on a 333 ms budget. What it costs is standing power and heat
on a battery that warns at 11.1 V and cuts at 10.8 V, on a SoC that responds
to heat by throttling, which is the thing the pin was supposed to prevent.

Burning battery for inference the mission does not need is a real cost, and a
tool that silently reconfigures the SoC on every boot is not what this project
wants. So: report by default, `--apply` to change, /run (tmpfs) to remember
the old value, and a reboot puts it back on its own.

Pin it for a bench run. Do not pin it for a mission unless a measurement says
the ramp is a material part of the latency budget.


EVERY PATH BELOW IS UNVERIFIED
==============================
The board has not arrived. Thermal zone indices, the rknpu debugfs layout and
the devfreq node name all vary by BSP kernel version and none of them have
been seen on this hardware. Nothing here is hardcoded: every node is
discovered at runtime, every absence degrades to "unknown" rather than to a
plausible number, and `fpms-npu-tune --verify` prints the exact command that
confirms each one. When you have the board, run it and paste the output into
npu/README.md.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import signal
import sys
import time

TOOL = "fpms-npu-tune"
VERSION = "1"

CONFIG = "/etc/fpms/config.env"
RUN_DIR = "/run/fpms"
REPORT_JSON = "/run/fpms/npu-tune.json"       # agent 7's selftest may read this
MASK_ENV = "/run/fpms/npu.env"                # FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=<n>, tmpfs
RESTORE_JSON = "/run/fpms/npu-tune-restore.json"

THERMAL_ROOT = "/sys/class/thermal"
DEVFREQ_ROOT = "/sys/class/devfreq"
RKNPU_DEBUG = "/sys/kernel/debug/rknpu"
COMPATIBLE = "/proc/device-tree/compatible"

# THE VOCABULARY THE DAEMON ACTUALLY ACCEPTS.
#
# This is not our choice to make. fpms-npud (agent 2) owns the mechanism, and
# its resolve_core_mask() takes NAMED TOKENS, not bit masks:
#
#     table = {"0": NPU_CORE_0, "0_1": NPU_CORE_0_1,
#              "0_1_2": NPU_CORE_0_1_2, "auto": NPU_CORE_AUTO}
#
# and anything else is a named refusal that stops the daemon. So
# FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=7 -- the obvious spelling if you are thinking in bit
# masks, which is how the kernel and this tool think -- is REFUSED. Policy
# speaks the mechanism's language, not the other way round.
#
# VERIFIED by reading overlay/usr/local/bin/fpms-npud on 2026-08-08. Re-check
# if that file changes:  grep -A8 'table = {' /usr/local/bin/fpms-npud
#
# Note "0" here means NPU_CORE_0 (core 0 only), NOT the integer 0. The integer
# 0 is NPU_CORE_AUTO, which this table spells "auto". They are different
# things and the collision is exactly why nothing below is a bare number.
NPUD_TOKENS = {
    "0":     (1, "core 0 only"),
    "0_1":   (3, "cores 0+1"),
    "0_1_2": (7, "cores 0+1+2 (all three)"),
    # RKNN's own AUTO. In Rockchip's C API RKNN_NPU_CORE_AUTO means
    # "automatically pick an IDLE core" -- singular. Which core is chosen is a
    # runtime decision, so there is no fixed bit mask to report.
    # VERIFY ON HARDWARE:
    #   python3 -c "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite as R; print(R.NPU_CORE_AUTO)"
    "auto":  (None, "RKNN NPU_CORE_AUTO - ONE idle core, chosen at runtime"),
}

# Turning the policy's answer back into the daemon's language. Only these three
# masks are expressible; the daemon has no token for "core 2 only".
TOKEN_FOR_MASK = {1: "0", 3: "0_1", 7: "0_1_2"}

# Spellings an operator might plausibly reach for. fpms-npud accepts NONE of
# them. They are mapped here only so the report can say what was probably
# meant before it says the daemon will refuse to start.
ALIASES = {
    "1": 1, "0x1": 1, "core0": 1,
    "3": 3, "0x3": 3, "core0_1": 3,
    "7": 7, "0x7": 7, "core0_1_2": 7, "all": 7,
}

# Fallbacks used ONLY when the kernel exposes no trip points to discover.
# Marked because they are inferred from RK3588 datasheet-class numbers, not
# measured on this board.
FALLBACK_WARN_C = 80.0        # VERIFY ON HARDWARE
FALLBACK_CRIT_C = 95.0        # VERIFY ON HARDWARE

OK, DEGRADED, FAULT = "ok", "degraded", "fault"

_HAVE_ALARM = hasattr(signal, "SIGALRM")


def log(msg):
    """One line, prefixed, flushed. journald gets these verbatim."""
    sys.stdout.write("%s: %s\n" % (TOOL, msg))
    sys.stdout.flush()


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bounded sysfs I/O
#
# A sysfs or debugfs read hits driver code. A wedged rknpu driver can make one
# block, and this process runs forever and may be ordered before the inference
# daemon -- so an unbounded read here is a boot hazard. Every read is fenced
# with SIGALRM.
#
# Honest limit: SIGALRM cannot interrupt a task in uninterruptible sleep. If
# the driver hangs in D state no userspace timeout saves us, which is exactly
# why the unit sits on nothing's critical path and why the one place systemd
# orders anything after us carries a bounded TimeoutStartSec.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
class _ReadTimeout(Exception):
    pass


def _on_alarm(signum, frame):
    raise _ReadTimeout()


def read_text(path, timeout=1.0, limit=65536):
    """Return the file's contents stripped, or None. Never raises, never hangs
    on an interruptible read."""
    prev = None
    try:
        if _HAVE_ALARM:
            prev = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, _on_alarm)
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, timeout)
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            return fh.read(limit).decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - absence and timeout are both just "no data"
        return None
    finally:
        if _HAVE_ALARM and prev is not None:
            try:
                signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
                signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, prev)
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                pass


def read_int(path, timeout=1.0):
    txt = read_text(path, timeout)
    if txt is None:
        return None
    m = re.search(r"-?\d+", txt)
    return int(m.group(0)) if m else None


def write_text(path, value, timeout=2.0):
    """Returns (ok, error-string). The only function in this file that writes
    to the kernel, and it is only ever reached under --apply."""
    prev = None
    try:
        if _HAVE_ALARM:
            prev = signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, _on_alarm)
            signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, timeout)
        with open(path, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(value)
        return True, ""
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        return False, str(exc)
    finally:
        if _HAVE_ALARM and prev is not None:
            try:
                signal.setitimer(signal.ITIMER_REAL, 0)
                signal.signal(signal.SIGALRM, prev)
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                pass


def listdir(path):
    try:
        return sorted(os.listdir(path))
    except OSError:
        return []


def load_config():
    """Same shallow parse fpms-selftest uses. config.env is owned elsewhere;
    this tool reads it and never writes it."""
    cfg = {}
    try:
        with open(CONFIG) as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line or line.startswith("#") or "=" not in line:
                    continue
                k, v = line.split("=", 1)
                cfg[k.strip()] = v.strip().strip('"').strip("'")
    except OSError:
        pass
    # A value already in our environment (unit Environment=, or the operator's
    # shell) beats the file, because that is what the daemon would actually see.
    for k in ("FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK", "FPMS_NPU_MAX_LATENCY_MS",
              "FPMS_NPU_TUNE_INTERVAL_S", "FPMS_NPU_TUNE_GOVERNOR",
              "FPMS_NPU_TUNE_HEARTBEAT_S"):
        if os.environ.get(k):
            cfg[k] = os.environ[k]
    return cfg


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Discovery: thermal
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def scale_temp(raw):
    """Thermal zones report millidegrees C on every kernel we know of, but the
    unit is not guaranteed by the sysfs ABI and some vendor drivers report
    whole degrees. Scale by magnitude rather than by assumption; 1000 'degrees'
    is not a temperature this SoC survives.
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone0/temp"""
    if raw is None:
        return None
    return raw / 1000.0 if abs(raw) >= 1000 else float(raw)


def discover_thermal_zones():
    """Enumerate every thermal zone with its type, temperature and trip points.

    Zone INDICES ARE NOT STABLE and must never be hardcoded: RK3588 BSP kernels
    have shipped soc-thermal at zone0 in one release and gpu-thermal at zone0
    in another. Match on the `type` string, which is at least descriptive.
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type"""
    zones = []
    for name in listdir(THERMAL_ROOT):
        if not name.startswith("thermal_zone"):
            continue
        base = os.path.join(THERMAL_ROOT, name)
        ztype = read_text(os.path.join(base, "type"))
        temp = scale_temp(read_int(os.path.join(base, "temp")))
        if ztype is None and temp is None:
            continue
        trips = []
        for f in listdir(base):
            m = re.match(r"trip_point_(\d+)_temp$", f)
            if not m:
                continue
            t = scale_temp(read_int(os.path.join(base, f)))
            ttype = read_text(os.path.join(base, "trip_point_%s_type" % m.group(1)))
            if t is not None:
                trips.append({"index": int(m.group(1)), "temp_c": round(t, 1),
                              "type": ttype})
        trips.sort(key=lambda t: t["temp_c"])
        zones.append({
            "node": name,
            "type": ztype,
            "temp_c": None if temp is None else round(temp, 1),
            "trips": trips,
        })
    return zones


def pick_zone(zones, *needles):
    """First zone whose type contains any of the needles. Returns None rather
    than a substitute -- reporting the GPU's temperature as the NPU's would be
    a fabricated measurement, and this project has been hurt by those."""
    for needle in needles:
        for z in zones:
            if z["type"] and needle in z["type"].lower():
                return z
    return None


def discover_cooling_devices():
    """Cooling devices are the only NON-INFERRED evidence of throttling: a
    cur_state above 0 means the thermal governor is actively holding something
    down. A frequency below max proves nothing on its own -- an idle devfreq
    governor also clocks down, and confusing the two is how 'it throttles'
    becomes an unfalsifiable claim.
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  grep . /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/type"""
    devs = []
    for name in listdir(THERMAL_ROOT):
        if not name.startswith("cooling_device"):
            continue
        base = os.path.join(THERMAL_ROOT, name)
        devs.append({
            "node": name,
            "type": read_text(os.path.join(base, "type")),
            "cur_state": read_int(os.path.join(base, "cur_state")),
            "max_state": read_int(os.path.join(base, "max_state")),
        })
    return devs


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Discovery: DVFS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def discover_npu_devfreq():
    """Find the NPU's devfreq node.

    The directory is named after the device tree node -- on RK3588 BSP kernels
    that has been seen as `fdab0000.npu`, but the address is SoC- and
    DTS-dependent and RK3588S is not required to match RK3588. Match on 'npu'
    appearing in the node name or in the resolved device path instead.
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  ls -l /sys/class/devfreq/"""
    for name in listdir(DEVFREQ_ROOT):
        base = os.path.join(DEVFREQ_ROOT, name)
        real = ""
        try:
            real = os.path.realpath(base)
        except OSError:
            pass
        if "npu" not in name.lower() and "npu" not in real.lower():
            continue

        def hz(f):
            return read_int(os.path.join(base, f))

        avail = read_text(os.path.join(base, "available_frequencies")) or ""
        govs = read_text(os.path.join(base, "available_governors")) or ""
        return {
            "node": name,
            "path": base,
            "cur_hz": hz("cur_freq"),
            "min_hz": hz("min_freq"),
            "max_hz": hz("max_freq"),
            "governor": read_text(os.path.join(base, "governor")),
            "available_governors": govs.split(),
            # The DVFS operating points, lowest first. These are the OPPs the
            # governor may choose between; an empty list means the kernel is
            # not exposing them, NOT that the NPU has one frequency.
            "opps_hz": sorted(int(x) for x in avail.split() if x.isdigit()),
        }
    return None


def discover_rknpu_debug():
    """/sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/{version,load,freq,...}

    debugfs is root-only and may not be mounted at all. The layout is driver-
    version specific: `load` has shipped both as a single percentage and as a
    per-core list, and `freq`/`volt`/`power` exist on some driver versions and
    not others. Read what is there, claim nothing about what is not.
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:
      sudo ls /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/
      sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version
      sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load"""
    info = {
        "path": RKNPU_DEBUG,
        "present": os.path.isdir(RKNPU_DEBUG),
        "readable": None,
        "version": None,
        "load_raw": None,
        "freq_raw": None,
        "files": [],
    }
    if not info["present"]:
        info["readable"] = False
        # Distinguish "no debugfs" from "no rknpu". They need different fixes:
        # one is a mount, the other is a missing/failed driver.
        info["debugfs_mounted"] = os.path.isdir("/sys/kernel/debug")
        return info
    info["debugfs_mounted"] = True
    info["files"] = listdir(RKNPU_DEBUG)
    info["version"] = read_text(os.path.join(RKNPU_DEBUG, "version"))
    info["load_raw"] = read_text(os.path.join(RKNPU_DEBUG, "load"))
    info["freq_raw"] = read_text(os.path.join(RKNPU_DEBUG, "freq"))
    info["readable"] = any(v is not None for v in
                           (info["version"], info["load_raw"], info["freq_raw"]))
    return info


def parse_load(raw):
    """Per-core utilisation out of `/sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load`.

    Two formats have shipped and this tool must not care which it meets:
      'NPU load:  Core0:  0%, Core1:  0%, Core2:  0%,'   (RK3588 multi-core)
      'NPU load:  12%'                                    (single-core drivers)
    Returns (per_core_list_or_None, overall_or_None).
    VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load"""
    if not raw:
        return None, None
    cores = re.findall(r"Core\s*(\d+)\s*:\s*(\d+)\s*%", raw, re.IGNORECASE)
    if cores:
        out = [None] * (max(int(i) for i, _ in cores) + 1)
        for idx, pct in cores:
            out[int(idx)] = int(pct)
        known = [c for c in out if c is not None]
        return out, (max(known) if known else None)
    single = re.search(r"(\d+)\s*%", raw)
    if single:
        return None, int(single.group(1))
    return None, None


def detect_core_count(rknpu, devfreq):
    """How many NPU cores does this SoC actually have?

    Preference order is deliberate: an observed per-core load list is
    EVIDENCE; the SoC compatible string is an INFERENCE. Returns
    (count_or_None, how_we_know)."""
    per_core, _ = parse_load(rknpu.get("load_raw"))
    if per_core:
        return len(per_core), "observed in %s/load" % RKNPU_DEBUG

    compat = read_text(COMPATIBLE) or ""
    compat = compat.replace("\x00", " ").lower()
    if "rk3588" in compat:
        # RK3588 and RK3588S both carry the same 3-core 6 TOPS NPU. Inferred
        # from the part, not seen on this board.
        # VERIFY ON HARDWARE:  tr '\\0' '\\n' < /proc/device-tree/compatible
        return 3, "inferred from SoC compatible %r (VERIFY ON HARDWARE)" % compat.strip()

    if devfreq:
        return None, "devfreq node %s present but core count not exposed" % devfreq["node"]
    return None, "no evidence available"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Policy: the core mask
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def resolve_core_mask(cfg, core_count, core_count_how):
    """Decide what FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK should be, in the daemon's vocabulary.

    See the module docstring for the reasoning. In one line: FPMS runs one
    model at ~3 inferences/second, so there is no concurrency for per-core
    throughput mode to exploit, and the only lever is single-inference latency
    -> spread the one model across every core the SoC has.

    Returns both the token fpms-npud accepts and the bit mask it implies. The
    token is what gets written anywhere; the mask exists so this tool can ask
    whether the SoC actually has the cores being named."""
    raw_in = cfg.get("FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK")
    raw = (raw_in or "").strip().lower()
    out = {
        "configured": raw_in if raw_in is not None else "(unset)",
        "token": None,          # what fpms-npud will be given
        "resolved": None,       # the bit mask it implies, or None for auto
        "resolved_hex": None,
        "label": None,
        "policy": "latency (one model, ~3 inferences/s, no concurrency to win)",
        "source": None,
        # Each warning carries its own severity. A mask that names a core the
        # SoC does not have is a FAULT (init_runtime() fails and the agent
        # swallows it); a mask that merely wastes cores is DEGRADED.
        "warnings": [],
        "measured": False,   # nothing here has run on an RK3588S. MEASURE ME.
    }

    def warn(sev, text):
        out["warnings"].append({"severity": sev, "text": text})

    if raw == "":
        # NOTHING CONFIGURED -> the policy decides. This is the only branch
        # where this tool chooses; every other branch reports someone else's
        # choice back to them.
        if core_count is None:
            # Refuse to guess wide. A token naming a core the SoC does not
            # have makes init_runtime() fail, and fpms_rover_agent.py swallows
            # that and streams video with no detection -- the worst failure
            # mode in the project. Core 0 only is never worse than the
            # behaviour we are replacing.
            out["token"], out["resolved"] = "0", 1
            out["source"] = "policy FALLBACK: core count unknown (%s)" % core_count_how
            warn(DEGRADED,
                 "Could not establish how many NPU cores this SoC has, so the "
                 "policy fell back to core 0 only -- one third of the NPU. "
                 "This is not a failure of the NPU, it is a failure to READ "
                 "it: debugfs is root-only and may not be mounted. Run "
                 "`sudo fpms-npu-tune` and `sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load`.")
        else:
            mask = (1 << core_count) - 1
            token = TOKEN_FOR_MASK.get(mask)
            if token is None:
                # More cores than fpms-npud has a token for. Name the most
                # cores it CAN express rather than inventing a spelling it
                # will refuse.
                token, mask = "0_1_2", 7
                warn(DEGRADED,
                     "This SoC reports %d NPU cores, but fpms-npud's "
                     "vocabulary only reaches 0_1_2 (three). Using 0_1_2; "
                     "cores beyond the third are unused." % core_count)
            out["token"], out["resolved"] = token, mask
            out["source"] = ("policy: FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK unset -> all %d cores "
                             "(%s)" % (core_count, core_count_how))
    elif raw in NPUD_TOKENS:
        out["resolved"], out["label"] = NPUD_TOKENS[raw]
        out["token"] = raw
        out["source"] = "explicit FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s" % raw
    elif raw in ALIASES:
        # Reports what they meant, then says the daemon will not accept it.
        mask = ALIASES[raw]
        out["resolved"] = mask
        out["token"] = TOKEN_FOR_MASK.get(mask)
        out["source"] = "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s (spelling fpms-npud REFUSES)" % raw
        warn(FAULT,
             "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%r is a bit-mask spelling. fpms-npud takes "
             "NAMED TOKENS and refuses anything else, so the daemon will not "
             "start with this set. It looks like you meant %s -- write "
             "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s instead."
             % (raw, TOKEN_FOR_MASK.get(mask, "?"), TOKEN_FOR_MASK.get(mask, "0_1_2")))
    else:
        out["source"] = "unparseable FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%r" % raw
        warn(FAULT,
             "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%r is not one of fpms-npud's tokens, so the "
             "daemon refuses it and does not start -- the rover streams video "
             "and detects nothing. Accepted: 0 | 0_1 | 0_1_2 | auto. Leave it "
             "unset to let this tool's policy choose." % raw)

    mask = out["resolved"]
    out["resolved_hex"] = None if mask is None else "0x%x" % mask
    if out["label"] is None:
        out["label"] = ("unknown" if mask is None
                        else dict((v[0], v[1]) for v in NPUD_TOKENS.values()
                                  if v[0] is not None).get(mask, "mask 0x%x" % mask))

    # The trap. In fpms-npud's vocabulary the dangerous value is "auto", NOT
    # "0" -- "0" there means NPU_CORE_0, one named core, which at least says
    # what it does. "auto" is RKNN's NPU_CORE_AUTO: one IDLE core, singular,
    # while reading like "let the system use everything".
    if raw == "auto":
        warn(FAULT,
             "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=auto is RKNN's NPU_CORE_AUTO, which selects "
             "ONE IDLE CORE -- it does not mean 'all cores'. With a single "
             "model this leaves two thirds of a 6 TOPS NPU idle while the "
             "config line reads as though the question had been settled. Use "
             "0_1_2, or leave it unset and let this tool's policy resolve it.")
    elif mask is not None and core_count and mask >= (1 << core_count):
        warn(FAULT,
             "%s names a core this SoC does not have (%d cores detected). "
             "init_runtime() will FAIL, and fpms_rover_agent.py swallows that "
             "failure and streams video with no detection."
             % (out["token"] or "this mask", core_count))
    elif mask is not None and core_count and bin(mask).count("1") == 1 and core_count > 1:
        warn(DEGRADED,
             "Token %s uses a single core of %d. That is the right choice ONLY "
             "if a measurement showed multi-core latency was worse for this "
             "model -- record the two numbers next to the setting if so."
             % (out["token"], core_count))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Snapshot
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def snapshot(cfg):
    zones = discover_thermal_zones()
    cooling = discover_cooling_devices()
    devfreq = discover_npu_devfreq()
    rknpu = discover_rknpu_debug()
    core_count, how = detect_core_count(rknpu, devfreq)
    mask = resolve_core_mask(cfg, core_count, how)
    per_core, overall = parse_load(rknpu.get("load_raw"))

    npu_zone = pick_zone(zones, "npu")
    soc_zone = pick_zone(zones, "soc", "cpu", "center", "package")
    temps = [z["temp_c"] for z in zones if z["temp_c"] is not None]
    hottest = max(temps) if temps else None
    hottest_zone = None
    if hottest is not None:
        for z in zones:
            if z["temp_c"] == hottest:
                hottest_zone = z["type"] or z["node"]
                break

    # Thresholds come from the kernel's own trip points where they exist.
    # Discovering them beats hardcoding: the BSP knows this SoC's limits and
    # this file does not.
    warn_c, crit_c, thresh_src = FALLBACK_WARN_C, FALLBACK_CRIT_C, "fallback (VERIFY ON HARDWARE)"
    ref = npu_zone or soc_zone
    if ref and ref["trips"]:
        passive = [t["temp_c"] for t in ref["trips"]
                   if (t["type"] or "").lower() in ("passive", "hot", "active")]
        critical = [t["temp_c"] for t in ref["trips"]
                    if (t["type"] or "").lower() == "critical"]
        if passive:
            warn_c = min(passive)
        if critical:
            crit_c = min(critical)
        thresh_src = "trip points on %s" % (ref["type"] or ref["node"])

    # Throttling: only assert it on cooling-device evidence. Otherwise null.
    active_cool = [d for d in cooling
                   if d["cur_state"] not in (None, 0) and (d["max_state"] or 0) > 0]
    if cooling:
        throttling = bool(active_cool)
    else:
        throttling = None

    freq_pct = None
    if devfreq and devfreq["cur_hz"] and devfreq["max_hz"]:
        freq_pct = round(100.0 * devfreq["cur_hz"] / devfreq["max_hz"], 1)

    snap = {
        "tool": TOOL,
        "version": VERSION,
        "ts": time.time(),
        "ts_iso": time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S", time.localtime()),
        "root": os.geteuid() == 0 if hasattr(os, "geteuid") else None,
        "core_mask": mask,
        "cores": {"count": core_count, "how": how},
        "driver_version": rknpu.get("version"),
        "load": {
            "path": os.path.join(RKNPU_DEBUG, "load"),
            "per_core_pct": per_core,
            "overall_pct": overall,
            "raw": rknpu.get("load_raw"),
        },
        "thermal": {
            "zones": zones,
            "cooling_devices": cooling,
            "npu_c": npu_zone["temp_c"] if npu_zone else None,
            "npu_zone": (npu_zone or {}).get("type"),
            "soc_c": soc_zone["temp_c"] if soc_zone else None,
            "soc_zone": (soc_zone or {}).get("type"),
            "hottest_c": hottest,
            "hottest_zone": hottest_zone,
            "warn_c": warn_c,
            "crit_c": crit_c,
            "thresholds_from": thresh_src,
            "throttling": throttling,
            "active_cooling": [d["type"] for d in active_cool],
        },
        "dvfs": devfreq or {"node": None, "path": None},
        "dvfs_pct_of_max": freq_pct,
        "debugfs": {k: rknpu[k] for k in ("present", "readable", "files",
                                          "debugfs_mounted") if k in rknpu},
        "max_latency_ms": cfg.get("FPMS_NPU_MAX_LATENCY_MS"),
        "issues": [],
        "status": OK,
    }
    grade(snap)
    return snap


def grade(snap):
    """Turn the snapshot into named issues. Absence of a reading is DEGRADED,
    never OK: 'I could not measure it' and 'it is fine' are different answers
    and this project has repeatedly been burned by treating them as the same."""
    issues = snap["issues"]

    def add(sev, name, detail, remedy=""):
        issues.append({"severity": sev, "issue": name,
                       "detail": detail, "remedy": remedy})

    for w in snap["core_mask"]["warnings"]:
        add(w["severity"], "core mask", w["text"],
            "Set FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK in /etc/fpms/config.env, then restart "
            "fpms-npud. `fpms-npu-tune --print-env` prints the line.")

    if not snap["debugfs"].get("present"):
        if snap["debugfs"].get("debugfs_mounted") is False:
            add(DEGRADED, "debugfs not mounted",
                "/sys/kernel/debug is not mounted, so per-core NPU load and "
                "the driver version cannot be read at all.",
                "sudo mount -t debugfs none /sys/kernel/debug")
        else:
            add(FAULT, "rknpu driver absent",
                "%s does not exist. debugfs is mounted, so this is the rknpu "
                "kernel driver missing or failed to probe -- not a mount "
                "problem. There is no mainline rknpu driver; this must be the "
                "Rockchip BSP kernel." % RKNPU_DEBUG,
                "uname -a; dmesg | grep -i rknpu")
    elif snap["debugfs"].get("readable") is False:
        add(DEGRADED, "debugfs unreadable",
            "%s exists but nothing in it could be read. debugfs is root-only."
            % RKNPU_DEBUG,
            "Re-run as root: sudo fpms-npu-tune")

    if snap["driver_version"] is None and snap["debugfs"].get("readable"):
        add(DEGRADED, "driver version unknown",
            "%s/version did not read. The runtime, the wheel and the driver "
            "must be version-matched; a mismatch fails at init_runtime() with "
            "a message the agent swallows." % RKNPU_DEBUG,
            "sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version; "
            "cat /etc/fpms/npu-versions.json")

    if not snap["thermal"]["zones"]:
        add(DEGRADED, "no thermal zones",
            "%s exposed no zones. RK3588 throttles hard and silently; with no "
            "zones there is no way to know whether a latency number was taken "
            "hot or cold." % THERMAL_ROOT,
            "cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type")
    else:
        hot = snap["thermal"]["hottest_c"]
        if hot is not None:
            if hot >= snap["thermal"]["crit_c"]:
                add(FAULT, "SoC at critical temperature",
                    "%s is %.1f C, at or past the critical trip %.1f C (%s)."
                    % (snap["thermal"]["hottest_zone"], hot,
                       snap["thermal"]["crit_c"], snap["thermal"]["thresholds_from"]),
                    "Stop the mission and let it cool. Inference latency is "
                    "already unrepresentative at this temperature.")
            elif hot >= snap["thermal"]["warn_c"]:
                add(DEGRADED, "SoC hot",
                    "%s is %.1f C, past the passive trip %.1f C (%s). Any "
                    "latency measured now is a throttled number."
                    % (snap["thermal"]["hottest_zone"], hot,
                       snap["thermal"]["warn_c"], snap["thermal"]["thresholds_from"]),
                    "Expect the governor to clock down. Re-bench cold before "
                    "concluding anything about the model.")
        if snap["thermal"]["npu_c"] is None:
            add(DEGRADED, "no NPU thermal zone",
                "No thermal zone's type contains 'npu'. Reporting another "
                "zone's temperature as the NPU's would be a fabricated "
                "measurement, so NPU temperature is reported as unknown.",
                "cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type   # find the real name")

    if snap["thermal"]["throttling"] is True:
        add(DEGRADED, "actively throttling",
            "Cooling devices engaged: %s. Frequency is being held down by the "
            "thermal governor RIGHT NOW."
            % ", ".join(snap["thermal"]["active_cooling"] or ["(unnamed)"]),
            "Any inference latency measured in this state is a throttled "
            "number, not the model's latency.")

    if snap["dvfs"].get("node") is None:
        add(DEGRADED, "no NPU devfreq node",
            "No devfreq node matched 'npu' under %s, so NPU frequency, the "
            "available operating points and the governor are all unknown. The "
            "node name is DTS-dependent and was never confirmed on this board."
            % DEVFREQ_ROOT,
            "ls -l /sys/class/devfreq/")
    else:
        if not snap["dvfs"].get("opps_hz"):
            add(DEGRADED, "no DVFS operating points exposed",
                "%s/available_frequencies is empty or absent. This does NOT "
                "mean the NPU has one frequency -- it means the kernel is not "
                "telling us." % snap["dvfs"]["path"],
                "cat %s/available_frequencies" % snap["dvfs"]["path"])

    # Worst severity wins. No issues at all is the only way to reach "ok" --
    # "I could not measure it" is DEGRADED, never OK.
    if any(i["severity"] == FAULT for i in issues):
        snap["status"] = FAULT
    elif issues:
        snap["status"] = DEGRADED
    else:
        snap["status"] = OK


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Human report
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def mhz(hz):
    return "?" if not hz else "%d MHz" % round(hz / 1e6)


def print_report(snap):
    t = snap["thermal"]
    d = snap["dvfs"]
    m = snap["core_mask"]

    print("")
    print("  %s  %s   status: %s" % (TOOL, snap["ts_iso"], snap["status"].upper()))
    if snap.get("root") is False:
        print("  (not root: debugfs is root-only, so per-core load and the "
              "driver version will read as unknown)")
    print("")

    print("  CORES")
    print("    detected        %s   (%s)"
          % (snap["cores"]["count"] if snap["cores"]["count"] else "unknown",
             snap["cores"]["how"]))
    print("    configured      FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s" % m["configured"])
    print("    resolves to     %s   (%s%s)"
          % (m["token"] or "REFUSED", m["label"],
             "" if not m["resolved_hex"] else ", mask %s" % m["resolved_hex"]))
    print("    policy          %s" % m["policy"])
    print("    measured?       NO - nothing here has run on an RK3588S. MEASURE ME.")
    if snap["load"]["per_core_pct"]:
        print("    load            %s"
              % "  ".join("core%d %s%%" % (i, "?" if v is None else v)
                          for i, v in enumerate(snap["load"]["per_core_pct"])))
    elif snap["load"]["overall_pct"] is not None:
        print("    load            %d%% (driver reports no per-core breakdown)"
              % snap["load"]["overall_pct"])
    else:
        print("    load            unknown (%s unreadable)" % snap["load"]["path"])
    print("    driver          %s" % (snap["driver_version"] or "unknown"))
    print("")

    print("  THERMAL     thresholds from: %s" % t["thresholds_from"])
    if not t["zones"]:
        print("    (no thermal zones found under %s)" % THERMAL_ROOT)
    for z in t["zones"]:
        flag = ""
        if z["temp_c"] is not None and z["temp_c"] >= t["crit_c"]:
            flag = "  <== CRITICAL"
        elif z["temp_c"] is not None and z["temp_c"] >= t["warn_c"]:
            flag = "  <== hot"
        print("    %-14s %-18s %s%s"
              % (z["node"], z["type"] or "?",
                 "?" if z["temp_c"] is None else "%.1f C" % z["temp_c"], flag))
    print("    npu             %s" % ("unknown - no zone type contains 'npu'"
                                      if t["npu_c"] is None else "%.1f C (%s)"
                                      % (t["npu_c"], t["npu_zone"])))
    print("    throttling      %s" % {True: "YES - " + ", ".join(t["active_cooling"] or ["?"]),
                                      False: "no (no cooling device engaged)",
                                      None: "unknown (no cooling devices exposed)"}[t["throttling"]])
    print("")

    print("  DVFS")
    if not d.get("node"):
        print("    (no devfreq node matched 'npu' under %s)" % DEVFREQ_ROOT)
    else:
        print("    node            %s" % d["node"])
        print("    frequency       %s  (min %s, max %s%s)"
              % (mhz(d["cur_hz"]), mhz(d["min_hz"]), mhz(d["max_hz"]),
                 "" if snap["dvfs_pct_of_max"] is None
                 else ", now %.0f%% of max" % snap["dvfs_pct_of_max"]))
        print("    governor        %s   available: %s"
              % (d["governor"] or "?", " ".join(d["available_governors"]) or "?"))
        print("    operating pts   %s"
              % (", ".join(mhz(f) for f in d["opps_hz"]) if d["opps_hz"]
                 else "not exposed by the kernel"))
    print("")

    if snap["issues"]:
        print("  ISSUES")
        for i in snap["issues"]:
            print("    [%s] %s" % (i["severity"].upper(), i["issue"]))
            print("           %s" % i["detail"])
            if i["remedy"]:
                print("           -> %s" % i["remedy"])
        print("")
    else:
        print("  ISSUES  none\n")

    print("  This tool changed nothing. `--apply` is required for that, and")
    print("  `fpms-npu-tune --verify` prints the commands that confirm every")
    print("  path above on real hardware.")
    print("")


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --verify: hand the operator the commands, do not make them trust this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFY_TEXT = """
Every path this tool reads was inferred from RK3588 BSP documentation and NOT
seen on this board. Run these on the Pi and paste the output into
/opt/fpms-os/npu/README.md so the next person does not have to guess.

  1. Thermal zone names and indices (indices are NOT stable across kernels)
       cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type
       paste -d' ' <(cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/type) \\
                   <(cat /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/temp)
     Expect a zone whose type mentions npu. If there is none, NPU temperature
     is genuinely unavailable -- do not substitute the GPU or SoC zone.

  2. Trip points, which is where this tool gets its warn/critical thresholds
       grep . /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/trip_point_*_temp
       grep . /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone*/trip_point_*_type

  3. Cooling devices -- the only non-inferred evidence of throttling
       grep . /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/type
       grep . /sys/class/thermal/cooling_device*/cur_state

  4. The NPU devfreq node. The directory name is DTS-dependent.
       ls -l /sys/class/devfreq/
       cat /sys/class/devfreq/*npu*/cur_freq
       cat /sys/class/devfreq/*npu*/available_frequencies
       cat /sys/class/devfreq/*npu*/available_governors
       cat /sys/class/devfreq/*npu*/governor
       cat /sys/class/devfreq/*npu*/trans_stat        # time spent per OPP

  5. rknpu debugfs. Root only, and the layout is driver-version specific.
       sudo ls  /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/
       sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/version
       sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/load
       sudo cat /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu/freq      # may not exist
     If /sys/kernel/debug/rknpu is missing, first check debugfs is mounted:
       mount | grep debugfs
     and only then conclude the driver is absent:
       dmesg | grep -i rknpu

  6. The core count and the mask constants, from the runtime itself
       tr '\\0' '\\n' < /proc/device-tree/compatible
       python3 -c "from rknnlite.api import RKNNLite as R; \\
                   print({k: getattr(R, k) for k in dir(R) if k.startswith('NPU_CORE')})"
     Confirm NPU_CORE_AUTO == 0 and NPU_CORE_0_1_2 == 7, and confirm that AUTO
     selects ONE idle core rather than all three -- that distinction is the
     reason FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=auto is a trap.

  7. That the value this tool resolves actually reaches the daemon
       systemctl cat  fpms-npud | grep EnvironmentFile
       systemctl show fpms-npud -p Environment
     The second command is the one that settles it. SPEC.md section 2 and
     fpms-npud.service's own header make OPPOSITE claims about whether a value
     in config.env overrides a later Environment= line; both cannot be right,
     and reading either file again will not tell you which. Measure it.

  8. The measurement that decides the mask policy (agent 7's bench)
       sudo FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=0     /opt/fpms-os/npu/bench/fpms_npu_bench.py
       sudo FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=0_1_2 /opt/fpms-os/npu/bench/fpms_npu_bench.py
     Tokens, not numbers: fpms-npud refuses a bit mask. Compare SUSTAINED
     latency percentiles, not the first inference. Run each long enough to
     reach thermal steady state and record the temperature alongside -- with
     `fpms-npu-tune --json` captured at the same moment -- or the numbers mean
     nothing.
"""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --apply
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def ensure_run_dir():
    try:
        os.makedirs(RUN_DIR, mode=0o755, exist_ok=True)
        return True
    except OSError as exc:
        log("cannot create %s: %s" % (RUN_DIR, exc))
        return False


def apply_core_mask(snap):
    """Write the resolved mask where the inference daemon can pick it up.

    This does NOT reconfigure the SoC -- a core mask is an argument to
    init_runtime() inside the daemon's own process, so no external tool can
    change it for a process that is already running. All this does is turn the
    policy's `auto` into a concrete number that a static config file cannot
    express, and put it in tmpfs where a reboot re-derives it.

    CROSS-AGENT CONTRACT -- AS CHECKED ON 2026-08-08, THIS FILE IS INERT.
    fpms-npud.service carries `EnvironmentFile=-/etc/fpms/config.env` and an
    `Environment=FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=0_1_2` default. It does NOT read
    /run/fpms/npu.env. For the runtime resolution to reach the daemon, someone
    who owns that unit must add

        EnvironmentFile=-/run/fpms/npu.env

    to it. Until then the authoritative setting is the explicit value in
    /etc/fpms/config.env, which `--print-env` prints ready to paste -- and the
    daemon's built-in default is 0_1_2, which already agrees with this policy,
    so the shipped behaviour is correct even with this file ignored. Re-check
    rather than trusting this comment:
        systemctl cat fpms-npud | grep EnvironmentFile

    And note an unresolved contradiction while you are there: SPEC.md section 2
    says EnvironmentFile= placed first means config.env CANNOT override the
    Environment= lines, while fpms-npud.service's own comment says the same
    ordering means config.env DOES win. Both cannot be true. Settle it by
    measurement, not by reading either file:
        systemctl show fpms-npud -p Environment
    """
    m = snap["core_mask"]
    if m["token"] is None:
        # Writing a value the daemon refuses would turn a config typo into a
        # daemon that will not start, with this tool's name on it.
        log("refusing to write a core mask: %s" % m["source"])
        for w in m["warnings"]:
            log("%s: %s" % (w["severity"].upper(), w["text"]))
        return 2
    if not ensure_run_dir():
        return 2
    body = (
        "# Written by %s at %s. tmpfs: re-derived every boot, never persisted.\n"
        "# Policy: one model at ~3 inferences/s has no concurrency for per-core\n"
        "# throughput mode, so spread it for latency. Resolved from: %s\n"
        "# Token vocabulary is fpms-npud's: 0 | 0_1 | 0_1_2 | auto. NOT a\n"
        "# bit mask -- the daemon refuses numbers.\n"
        "FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s\n"
        % (TOOL, snap["ts_iso"], m["source"], m["token"])
    )
    try:
        tmp = MASK_ENV + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            fh.write(body)
        os.replace(tmp, MASK_ENV)          # atomic: npud never reads a half file
        os.chmod(MASK_ENV, 0o644)
    except OSError as exc:
        log("FAILED to write %s: %s" % (MASK_ENV, exc))
        return 2

    log("core mask %s (%s) -> %s  [%s]"
        % (m["token"], m["label"], MASK_ENV, m["source"]))
    for w in m["warnings"]:
        log("%s: %s" % (w["severity"].upper(), w["text"]))
    return 0


POWER_WARNING = """
  POWER AND HEAT COST -- read before you leave this pinned
  -------------------------------------------------------
  A performance governor holds the NPU at maximum frequency CONTINUOUSLY,
  including the ~90% of every second that a 3-inference/second workload leaves
  it idle. What it buys is the DVFS ramp at the leading edge of an inference:
  microseconds to a few milliseconds, against a 333 ms budget.

  What it costs is standing power, on a battery whose pack thresholds are
  documented as 11.1 V warn and 10.8 V cut, and heat on a SoC that answers
  heat by throttling -- which is the thing the pin was meant to prevent.

  Pin it for a bench run. Do not leave it pinned for a mission unless a
  measurement says the ramp is a material part of the latency budget.

  This pin lives in tmpfs state and is NOT persisted: a reboot puts the
  governor back on its own. `fpms-npu-tune --apply --restore-governor` undoes
  it now.
"""


def apply_governor(snap, want):
    d = snap["dvfs"]
    if not d.get("node"):
        log("cannot set governor: no NPU devfreq node found under %s. "
            "Confirm with: ls -l /sys/class/devfreq/" % DEVFREQ_ROOT)
        return 2

    avail = d.get("available_governors") or []
    if avail and want not in avail:
        # Refuse rather than write a value the kernel will reject. A failed
        # write here is silent from the operator's side.
        log("refusing: governor %r is not in this kernel's list (%s)"
            % (want, " ".join(avail)))
        return 2

    current = d.get("governor")
    if current == want:
        log("governor already %s on %s - nothing to do" % (want, d["node"]))
        return 0

    if want == "performance":
        sys.stdout.write(POWER_WARNING)
        sys.stdout.flush()

    if ensure_run_dir() and current:
        # Record what it was, so --restore-governor is not a guess. tmpfs, so
        # the record dies with the pin it describes.
        try:
            with open(RESTORE_JSON, "w") as fh:
                json.dump({"node": d["node"], "path": d["path"],
                           "governor": current, "ts": snap["ts_iso"]}, fh, indent=2)
        except OSError as exc:
            log("WARNING: could not record the previous governor (%s). "
                "--restore-governor will not know what to put back; a reboot "
                "still will." % exc)

    ok, err = write_text(os.path.join(d["path"], "governor"), want)
    if not ok:
        log("FAILED to set governor to %s on %s: %s (root required)"
            % (want, d["node"], err))
        return 2
    now = read_text(os.path.join(d["path"], "governor"))
    log("governor %s -> %s on %s (readback: %s)"
        % (current or "?", want, d["node"], now or "?"))
    if now != want:
        log("WARNING: readback does not match. The kernel accepted the write "
            "and did something else -- treat the governor as UNKNOWN.")
        return 1
    return 0


def restore_governor(snap):
    rec = None
    try:
        with open(RESTORE_JSON) as fh:
            rec = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    if not rec:
        log("nothing to restore: no %s. Either the governor was never changed "
            "by this tool, or the machine has rebooted since -- a reboot "
            "already restored it." % RESTORE_JSON)
        return 0
    ok, err = write_text(os.path.join(rec["path"], "governor"), rec["governor"])
    if not ok:
        log("FAILED to restore governor %s on %s: %s"
            % (rec["governor"], rec["node"], err))
        return 2
    log("governor restored to %s on %s" % (rec["governor"], rec["node"]))
    try:
        os.unlink(RESTORE_JSON)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --watch: the unit's normal mode
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def temp_bucket(c, warn_c, crit_c):
    """Coarse, hysteresis-free-by-construction temperature state.

    The 5 C bucket is what stops this from logging every cycle forever: a SoC
    idling near a bucket edge would otherwise flap a line into the journal
    every few seconds, and this process runs for the whole life of the mission.
    Named bands are separated out because crossing a trip is worth a line even
    if the bucket did not change."""
    if c is None:
        return "unknown"
    if c >= crit_c:
        return "CRITICAL"
    if c >= warn_c:
        return "hot"
    return "%d-%dC" % (int(c // 5) * 5, int(c // 5) * 5 + 5)


def state_signature(snap):
    """The tuple whose change is worth a journal line. Deliberately excludes
    raw temperature and raw frequency -- both move constantly and neither
    changing by a degree is an event."""
    t = snap["thermal"]
    d = snap["dvfs"]
    load = snap["load"]["per_core_pct"]
    return (
        snap["status"],
        snap["cores"]["count"],
        snap["core_mask"]["token"],
        d.get("governor"),
        temp_bucket(t["hottest_c"], t["warn_c"], t["crit_c"]),
        t["throttling"],
        # "is anything on the NPU at all" -- not the value, just the fact.
        None if load is None else tuple(v is not None and v > 0 for v in load),
        bool(snap["debugfs"].get("readable")),
    )


def summary_line(snap):
    t = snap["thermal"]
    d = snap["dvfs"]
    load = snap["load"]["per_core_pct"]
    if load:
        loads = "load[" + "/".join("?" if v is None else str(v) for v in load) + "]%"
    elif snap["load"]["overall_pct"] is not None:
        loads = "load %d%%" % snap["load"]["overall_pct"]
    else:
        loads = "load unknown"
    return ("status=%s mask=%s(%s) cores=%s %s temp=%s gov=%s freq=%s throttle=%s"
            % (snap["status"],
               snap["core_mask"]["token"] or "REFUSED", snap["core_mask"]["label"],
               snap["cores"]["count"] or "?",
               loads,
               "?" if t["hottest_c"] is None
               else "%.1fC(%s)" % (t["hottest_c"], t["hottest_zone"]),
               d.get("governor") or "?",
               mhz(d.get("cur_hz")),
               {True: "YES", False: "no", None: "?"}[t["throttling"]]))


def publish(snap):
    """Write the JSON where agent 7's selftest can read it. tmpfs, atomic.
    A stale file is worse than no file, so `ts` is in the payload and the
    consumer is expected to check it."""
    if not ensure_run_dir():
        return
    try:
        tmp = REPORT_JSON + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w") as fh:
            json.dump(snap, fh, indent=2, default=str)
        os.replace(tmp, REPORT_JSON)
        os.chmod(REPORT_JSON, 0o644)
    except OSError:
        # Publishing is best-effort. A monitor that dies because it could not
        # write a report file is worse than one that keeps watching.
        pass


def watch(cfg):
    try:
        interval = max(2, int(float(cfg.get("FPMS_NPU_TUNE_INTERVAL_S", 10))))
    except ValueError:
        interval = 10
    try:
        heartbeat = int(float(cfg.get("FPMS_NPU_TUNE_HEARTBEAT_S", 0)))
    except ValueError:
        heartbeat = 0

    log("watching (interval %ds, heartbeat %s) - report only, changes nothing"
        % (interval, "off" if heartbeat <= 0 else "%ds" % heartbeat))

    last_sig = None
    last_beat = 0.0
    while True:
        try:
            snap = snapshot(cfg)
            publish(snap)
            sig = state_signature(snap)
            now = time.time()
            if sig != last_sig:
                log(summary_line(snap))
                # Only the issues that are new information; the summary line
                # already carries the state.
                for i in snap["issues"]:
                    if i["severity"] == FAULT:
                        log("  FAULT: %s - %s" % (i["issue"], i["detail"]))
                last_sig = sig
                last_beat = now
            elif heartbeat > 0 and (now - last_beat) >= heartbeat:
                log("(heartbeat) " + summary_line(snap))
                last_beat = now
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
            # A monitor must never be the thing that fails. Log once per
            # distinct message and keep going.
            msg = "monitor iteration failed: %r" % (exc,)
            if msg != last_sig:
                log(msg)
                last_sig = msg
        time.sleep(interval)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
def usage():
    print(__doc__.split("WHY THIS EXISTS")[0].strip())


def main(argv):
    args = argv[1:]

    if "-h" in args or "--help" in args:
        usage()
        return 0
    if "--verify" in args:
        print(VERIFY_TEXT)
        return 0

    cfg = load_config()

    if "--watch" in args:
        watch(cfg)          # never returns
        return 0

    snap = snapshot(cfg)

    if "--print-env" in args:
        m = snap["core_mask"]
        print("# Add to /etc/fpms/config.env (owned by the hardware agent -")
        print("# ask before editing it) and restart fpms-npud. Rationale:")
        print("# one model at ~3 inferences/s has no concurrency for per-core")
        print("# throughput mode, so spread the model for latency instead.")
        print("# Resolved from: %s" % m["source"])
        if m["token"] is None:
            print("# NO VALUE TO PRINT - the configured mask is one fpms-npud")
            print("# refuses. Accepted tokens: 0 | 0_1 | 0_1_2 | auto.")
            return 2
        # The token, never the bit mask: fpms-npud takes names and refuses
        # numbers, so `FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=7` would stop the daemon starting.
        print("FPMS_NPU_CORE_MASK=%s   # %s" % (m["token"], m["label"]))
        return 0

    if "--apply" in args:
        rc = 0
        did = False
        if "--core-mask" in args:
            rc = max(rc, apply_core_mask(snap))
            did = True
        if "--restore-governor" in args:
            rc = max(rc, restore_governor(snap))
            did = True
        if "--governor" in args:
            i = args.index("--governor")
            if i + 1 >= len(args):
                log("--governor needs a value, e.g. --governor performance")
                return 2
            rc = max(rc, apply_governor(snap, args[i + 1]))
            did = True
        if not did:
            log("--apply on its own changes nothing. Add --core-mask, "
                "--governor <name>, or --restore-governor.")
            return 2
        return rc

    # Report is the default, and reports write nothing.
    if "--json" in args:
        print(json.dumps(snap, indent=2, default=str))
    else:
        print_report(snap)

    return {OK: 0, DEGRADED: 1, FAULT: 2}[snap["status"]]


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(0)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        # Last line of defence. This binary can be on the boot path; it must
        # never take a traceback to systemd and it must never restart-loop on
        # a parsing bug against a sysfs layout nobody has seen yet.
        log("unhandled error (this tool changed nothing): %r" % (exc,))
        sys.exit(2)
